{
  "_title": "NOVA reference, single pass (A) with radiative extension",
  "_comment": "Architecture A on the NOVA reference engine: a 100 kN LOX/LH2 upper stage at Pc = 1000 psia, MR = 5.5, area ratio 40, with a single counterflow pass from an inlet manifold at an area ratio of three to the injector and a radiation-cooled C103 extension beyond it. The jacket is sixty circular GRCop-42 channels carrying hydrogen at 3.4 kg/s from 12 MPa and 30 K, with a hydrogen film at the chamber end, both volutes, and the exhaust plume at 5 kPa, its boundary and interior marched two lip radii past the exit plane. This is the configuration the guard tests in tests/testDefectGuards.py and the cooling-circuit verification scripts in validation/coolingCircuits run against, so each of them tests the jacket it was written for. Two of the settings are deliberate rather than arbitrary. The plume ambient is 5 kPa, near 20 km, because an area ratio of 40 separates internally below about 35 kPa and no attached plume model describes a separated nozzle; at sea level the plume solvers correctly refuse. The C103 extension reports a negative temperature margin and is included anyway: Bartz scales with chamber pressure to the 0.8, so a 6.9 MPa hydrogen engine puts a coated columbium shell over its limit at any area ratio, which is the result the solver exists to report rather than a fault in it, and the reason the shipped NOVANozzle.json carries its jacket to the exit on architecture B instead. Where A is viable: small chambers, and wherever the aft manifold's mass and size are acceptable. Heritage: the SSME nozzle tubes, fed at the exit, and NERVA.",
